            "who-can-use"
          ]
        }
      },
      "burnIns": [],
      "spoken": {
        "role": "scene",
        "chapter": "Chapter two. The constraints.",
        "narration": "The tool built for this is GitHub's merge queue. It gives the same safety as up to date, without making each author re-run. GitHub's own page says who gets it: public repositories owned by an organization, or private ones on Enterprise Cloud. Ours belong to a personal account, and our repository's rule settings, checked on November twenty-first, offer no merge queue at all."
      },
      "durationMs": 22300
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "picture": {
        "kind": "card"
      },
      "burnIns": [
        {
          "kind": "cheat-sheet",
          "params": {
            "kicker": "CONSTRAINTS",
            "title": "What a better pattern has to keep",
            "rows": [
              {
                "task": "GitHub",
                "command": "github.com",
                "note": "the code, the runs and the reviews stay there"
              },
              {
                "task": "Free",
                "command": "$0 a month",
                "note": "no paid plan, no runner minutes bought"
              },
              {
                "task": "Up to date",
                "command": "strict status checks",
                "note": "main is never red from two green branches"
              },
              {
                "task": "CI stays",
                "command": "CI Gate",
                "note": "it is what makes drift loud"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "spoken": {
        "role": "scene",
        "narration": "So the answer has to keep four things. It stays on GitHub. It costs nothing. Main never goes red because two green branches met for the first time after merging. And CI stays, because it is what makes drift loud, even on the days it finds nothing."
      },
      "durationMs": 21100
    },
    {
